  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated thunderstorms possible late this \nafternoon. Scattered showers regionwide tonight, with isolated \nthunderstorms possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA small, weak jet streak will develop aloft over the Gulf of\nMaine, with our forecast area sitting in the left exit region.\nThese dynamics will support the development of some brief vort\nmax late this afternoon which, in addition to peak diurnal \nheating, could provide enough lift and instability to support \nisolated to scattered showers and storms. Latest satellite and\nwebcams confirm the development of a cu field across the CWA\naway from the coast (seabreeze and stable). A few of these cu\nhave been getting high enough to produce some sprinkles or light\nshowers. So far nothing is high enough to produce lightning but\nwill give an isolated thunder threat the rest of the afternoon\nacross the central and northeast portions of the CWA. Showers\nand thunderstorms across Quebec will push towards the\nMaine/Quebec border around or after sunset tonight. This will\nlead to lessening thunder chances but scattered showers are\nexpected tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms on Friday, some of \nwhich may be strong with gusty winds and small hail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA stronger, more robust jet streak will develop aloft over the\nGulf of Maine on Friday, with our forecast area once again\nsitting in the left exit region. A cold front will push through\nthe forecast area on Friday, bringing synoptic lift strong\nenough for scattered to numerous showers and the development of\nthunderstorms by the afternoon.\n\nRecent runs of the HREF and REFS indicate moderate shear of 25\nto 35 kts, along with generous PWATs of 1.2 to 1.7 inches, with\nhigher moisture content being within the marine layer near the \ncoast. What seems more up in the air is the amount of CAPE, with\nthe HREF mean indicating 1000 to 1500 J/kg of SBCAPE, while the\nREFS has less with around 500 to 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE. The \namount of CAPE may come down to how much high cloud cover is in \nthe area through the day on Friday, with more cloud cover \npotentially limiting how much the atmosphere is able to charge \nbefore storms fire. With higher CAPE values, in combination with\nthe shear available, there could be the threat for some \nstronger thunderstorms that may produce gusty winds or even some\nsmall hail, though freezing levels may lift to 10-12kft, \nallowing for ample melting of any hail to push threats more in \nthe direction of gusty winds. Additionally, forecast profiles \nindicate most of the CAPE profile could be above the saturated \nlayer, which is another indicator that any strong to severe \nthreat will be in the form of wind. Have added enhanced wording\nof gusty winds in storms along a line from Dover-Foxcroft to\nBaxter to Houlton and points southward to the Airline (Route 9).\nThis area has the best chance of seeing high enough instability\nand best bulk shear environment. \n\nKey Message 3...System bringing rain showers and chance for\nthunder moves in Sunday afternoon. \n\nLow pressure system in Canada moves eastward this weekend, with\ntrough axis moving through Sunday afternoon. Associated cold \nfront moves through the state Sunday, funneling in some cooler \nair behind it. Expecting some showers to move into the north, \ngenerally expanding southeastward during the day Sunday.\nConfidence on how far south these showers get during the day is\nlow but the highest probabilities across the north. PWATs \ngenerally around 1-1.50 inches, so currently not anticipating \nany rain totals over one quarter inch. There will be a few \nhundred joules of CAPE, 40-50 kts of effective shear, and some \ndecent low level lapse rates. Combined, there is enough \ninstability that a thunderstorm is not out of the question. \nHeating ahead of the cold front in eastern Maine could aid in \ninitiating some convection along the boundary, but not \nanticipating severe storms at this time.",
